ge": "The timestamps in `events.csv` use three different timezone formats. Here are the whole file's three rows:\n\n```\nid,ts\n1,2026-03-01T10:00:00Z          \u2190 UTC\n2,2026-03-01T10:05:00           \u2190 no timezone (naive)\n3,2026-03-01T04:10:00-06:00     \u2190 UTC-6 offset\n```\n\n- **Row 1** has a `Z`, so it is 10:00 UTC.\n- **Row 3** has a `-06:00` offset. It reads as 04:10 on the clock, but that is 10:10 UTC.\n- **Row 2** has no timezone at all, so each tool has to guess. Pandas and JS `Date` treat it as local time or as UTC depending on the call. Excel ignores zones entirely. If your machine isn't on UTC, that row lands hours away from its neighbors.\n\nAll three events are probably within about 10 minutes of each other. A charting step that drops the offset and reads the clock time (04:10) will put row 3 six hours early. A step that reads row 2 as local time will shift it by your machine's UTC offset.\n\n**Fix:**\n1. Find out what zone the naive timestamps are in. The source system's docs or config should say. I can't tell from this file.\n2. Parse every value with an explicit zone. For example, in pandas use `pd.to_datetime(df.ts, utc=True)`. That only handles the naive rows correctly if they really are UTC. Otherwise, localize them to the right zone first.\n3. Better still, change the exporter to always write ISO 8601 with an offset or `Z`.\n\nI only looked at the data. I couldn't find any chart-building code in the repo, so I haven't confirmed which tool is doing the shifting. If you tell me what builds the charts, I can check how it parses these values. I can also write a normalizing script for the CSV if you tell me the zone of the naive rows.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}